WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.734
-Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:04.667
-Oh, I love that
little lapel pin.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.868
-Thank you. It's a mushroom.
-I love it. I get it.

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:08.934
I get it --
-Because --

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:10.868
-No, no, I understand.
-No, because --

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:12.133
-No, no, no. I understand.
Don't explain.

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:13.567
-Because --
-No, no, no.

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:15.834
You like mushrooms.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:17.701
-Yeah.
-That's right. Yeah.

00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:19.434
Last time you were here,
we had the best time.

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:21.467
I appreciate
you coming to the show.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.133
And you were very nervous
and excited

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.968
because your wife, Katherine,
was very pregnant

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:28.534
and was about to have a baby.
-That's right.

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:30.300
-And you had your phone out.
-Just in case.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.000
-You go, "Just in case
anything goes down

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.267
just during this interview."
And...?

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:38.567
-We had a deal
that if she was born

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.767
while I was on the show
that I would name her Jimmy.

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:42.334
-Questlove?

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:46.734
[ Drum roll]

00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:48.968
-She's named Eloise
'cause she was not born...

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:50.801
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah. Wow.
Beautiful. No, no, Beautiful.

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:54.234
[ Sad tuba plays ]
-Bohm-bohm! Boo, Eloise!

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:56.634
-No, no, no!
-One day you'll see this,

00:00:56.634 --> 00:00:59.601
and you'll know that I wish
your name was Jimmy...

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.167
-No, we love you.
-...not Eloise!

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:03.400
-Eloise.
Wait. That's beautiful.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.567
-Isn't that beautiful? Yeah.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:07.267
-You are now
the father of three.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.767
Does that ever sink in, bud?

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.100
-Wow.
-This is -- You're a dad.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.534
-Whew!
-Yeah. I mean...

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:13.300
-Yeah. Thank you.
-You're a fun dad.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.234
-I love it, man. Yeah, I have --

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.767
-You have a 10...
-I got a 10-year-old,

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:19.100
2 1/2, and 10 months.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.300
-Ooh!

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.734
The 10-year-old is my son Jack.
And then Lyla...

00:01:23.734 --> 00:01:25.100
-Oh, yeah. Lyla.
How's Lyla doing?

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.834
-Oh, my gosh. She's --

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.367
She's the best. She's so girly.
She's so cute.

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:34.667
She loves dresses and bows
and all of the princessy stuff.

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.601
Pink is her favorite color.
And she loves to chat.

00:01:36.601 --> 00:01:38.167
She's a real chatterbox.
She'll talk --

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.000
She was talking to
my sister on FaceTime,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.767
and then my sister
later said, she goes,

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:45.934
"Lyla is like the
personification of a Mad Lib

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:48.901
'cause she knows
all these words,

00:01:48.901 --> 00:01:51.033
but she doesn't quite know
how to really use them."

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.901
So she'll -- The other day,
she said, "Daddy, Daddy.

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:58.534
I'm going to Palm Beach
in two minutes!"

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:00.901
I said, "You are?"

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:04.934
She said, "Yes.
I'm going with Santa Claus.

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:08.033
We are going to bring
all of the sand."

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.267
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.767
-I was like,
"That's exactly a Mad Lib."

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:13.067
-Give me a noun. Give me a noun.
-Give me an adjective.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:15.033
-Yeah. And how's Grandpa?

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:17.067
Arnold Schwarzenegger
is your grandpa.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:18.834
-Yeah.
-He goes by "Opa."

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:20.968
-Oh.
-You know, he's Austrian.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:23.801
German. "Opa" is "grandfather"
in German.

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.133
So he goes by "Opa."

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:26.868
-[As Arnold]
Did he have a cigar?

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:28.100
-Oh. [ Laughs ]

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.501
-Did you have a cigar
with him?

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:30.968
-Yes. Yes.
-Yes. You had the cigar?

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:32.400
-Yeah.
-[Normal] He loves his cigar.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.033
-He loves his cigar.
-He came on the show.

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.400
He taught me
how to smoke a cigar.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.834
-Yeah, he kind of
taught me, too.

00:02:36.834 --> 00:02:39.634
I mean, there's nothing manlier
than Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:02:39.634 --> 00:02:41.434
smoking a cigar.
-Yes.

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:44.934
-And when you're part of,
you know, bringing

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.100
his granddaughter
into the world,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.534
he comes and he brings you
a cigar and he's like,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.033
"Let's go out and smoke these."

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.701
And I was like, "Okay,"
but I have asthma,

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:52.901
so I don't like cigars.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.334
And I was like,
"You got it, Arnold?

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:55.734
And Katherine's like,
"What are you doing?"

00:02:55.734 --> 00:02:57.367
I was like, "I'm fine.
I'm gonna be okay."

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.400
And so I go and I smoke
the cigar and I'm like,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.601
"This is good.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:03.033
[ Coughs ] Hmm.
Oh, yeah. Cohiba? Nice."

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:06.734
And then woke up the next day.
Massive lung infection.

00:03:06.734 --> 00:03:10.067
But -- Worth it.
-You always have to --

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:12.300
If Arnold says,
"Let's smoke a cigar..."

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.567
-You go, "Okay."
-Let's do it. Yeah.

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:15.868
You go, "Yeah."
-There's peer pressure,

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:17.601
and then there's...
-Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:03:17.601 --> 00:03:18.801
-...Arnold Schwarzenegger
pressure.

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:20.234
No, you do whatever --
He came on.

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:23.100
I said I want to understand it
and how to do it and everything,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.968
because I smoked
a couple of cigars.

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.334
I don't know when.

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:28.267
And I'm like --
You bite the end off

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:29.734
and then you, like,
lick the side.

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:31.300
-He goes by,
"You don't bite and the lick.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.868
What are you talking about?

00:03:32.868 --> 00:03:35.300
You don't lick the cigar!
You don't lick it and bite it!"

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.234
So I go -- I think I did.
He's like, "No!"

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:39.367
He goes, "Next time
I'm in town, I'll teach you.

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:41.767
I'll take you to a cigar bar,
and I'll teach you how."

00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.334
I go --
And he's a man of his word.

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:44.968
Called me on the phone.
-No.

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:47.501
-Said, "Jimmy, it's Arnold.
I'm going to a cigar bar."

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:49.234
And I was with --

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:51.467
I was with Drew Barrymore
and my wife, and I go,

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:53.300
"Do you guys want to go
to a cigar bar

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.801
with Arnold Schwarzenegger?"
They're like, "Let's do it."

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.968
We showed up,
and we all smoked cigars.

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.133
-Come on.
-It was the best.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.467
Love that dude.
Congrats, Opa. Opa?

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:02.701
-Opa Arnold.
-Congratulations, Opa.

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:05.000
-Stogies.
-Congrats to you and Katherine.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.667
-Thanks, man. Thank you.
Yeah, we're blessed.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:08.801
We feel very blessed.
-Good, nice parents.

00:04:08.801 --> 00:04:10.801
I want to talk "Super Mario
Bros." because that is --

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.868
That's the jam. I'm excited
about this Wednesday.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.567
-Let's go.

00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:15.701
-I'm excited.
-Go see it.

00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:16.934
-You're Mario.
-I'm Mario.

00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:18.634
I'm the voice of Mario.
-So what happens when

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.267
you get the phone call
and they're like,

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:23.234
"Hey, would you like
to be this iconic..."

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:25.400
-I'm like, "Pfft. No."

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.968
-Exactly.

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.133
-I was like,
"Oh, my God! Really?!"

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:30.834
Yeah, I mean,
you get that call.

00:04:30.834 --> 00:04:32.534
And it's just really exciting
because,

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:34.968
I mean, if you think about it,
all the IP that's out there,

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:37.267
anything that has reach,
it's already been remade.

00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:39.901
It's already been turned
into a TV show or a movie.

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:41.467
And for some reason.
the Super Mario Bros.

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:43.200
hasn't been a movie
for almost 30 years.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.133
And it's because I think
the folks at Nintendo

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.267
just didn't want to give
anybody the permission

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.667
to make the movie,
and so finally they made...

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.634
-Oh, Illumination is --
-Illumination, you know --

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:53.734
"Despicable Me."
"Secret Life of Pets."

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:55.901
"Minions." Brilliant.
-"Minions." Incredible studio.

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:57.868
And so they partnered up

00:04:57.868 --> 00:04:59.634
and they decided that they were
going to make this movie.

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:01.868
And so when something
like that comes around,

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.434
it's just like --
you're so blessed,

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:05.467
so honored to do it,
I mean, for me...

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:06.934
-It's fantastic.
It's gonna crush, buddy.

00:05:06.934 --> 00:05:08.367
And, you know,
I was thinking about you

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.267
because I was watching it,
and there's a lot of action.

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.601
There's a lot of action
in this movie.

00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:14.501
And I could just
kind of hear you --

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:17.033
a lot of grunting by you.

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.701
-Yes.
-Going like -- [ Grunting ]

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:21.000
And I go -- I was like --

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.334
"Oh, maybe Chris will bring
footage," of you doing

00:05:23.334 --> 00:05:24.701
those grunts
when you come on the show.

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.501
I just want to see you
in the sound booth going like...

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:27.834
[ Both grunting ]

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:29.968
-Well, you know, here's
an interesting thing

00:05:29.968 --> 00:05:31.968
about that, actually.

00:05:31.968 --> 00:05:34.200
Usually the effort sounds you do
at the end of the session.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.067
You probably do like
one or two sessions a month

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:37.434
over the course
of a couple years

00:05:37.434 --> 00:05:38.834
to voice one of these movies.

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:41.234
And at the end, because
the efforts and the grunts

00:05:41.234 --> 00:05:43.000
and the screaming, that really
takes it out of your voice,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.534
they save that for the end.

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:47.901
But here's an interesting
little tidbit.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.501
In a -- What makes
physical comedy work

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:52.667
in like a "Tom and Jerry"
or a "Super Mario Bros."

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:58.000
is when a character falls
a long way and hits,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.100
they don't die, right?
If they died, it would --

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.100
If Tom and Jerry --
If, like, Road Runner --

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.367
If, like, the anvil
hit his head

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:06.801
and there's freaking brains
everywhere...

00:06:06.801 --> 00:06:08.033
-No, no, no, no, no.
That'd be terrible.

00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:09.968
-Be like, "Oh, my God!"
That'd be bad, right?

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.501
-Terrible idea. Terrible.

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:16.100
-So in order to let kids know
that the character's not dead...

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.567
-Yes.
-...you have to do an,

00:06:18.567 --> 00:06:21.434
"I'm okay, I'm alive" sigh.

00:06:21.434 --> 00:06:23.200
"I'm hurt, not injured"
sigh.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.434
So it's like this.
You're like, "Aaaaaah!"

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.434
Unh!

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:28.467
Uhh...

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:30.133
-Yeah!

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:31.334
[ Applause ]

00:06:31.334 --> 00:06:34.100
-Without that...

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.067
-It just goes, hey,
it's a joke, everybody.

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:38.634
-Kids will be like,
"Mario is dead!

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:40.467
Mario fell to his death, Dad!"

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:42.267
-That's so funny.
It's so well-done.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:43.734
It's such a great cast.

00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:45.901
Everyone's so talented and good.
A lot of fun people.

